My Reply: 
I agree with you. The issue you raised is a common problem in smart smart.
Integer overflow is when a numeric value is stored in a memory location, and the value is read from that memory location. The problem arises because the value can be arbitrarily set, causing any user to have their balance set to any value.
The Integer Overflow is known when a contract's balance becomes 0, meaning that the contract has no more funds to pay the attacker.
The reason for the overflow is that the attacker can set any user's balance by sending the attacker's address as the target of the transferFrom function.
To fix this issue, we can use a safe spendOn function, which only allows the owner to spend on behalf of the user.
Also, we can use a burnFrom function to allow the owner to burn the user's tokens.
